            <ArticleTitle>Tadalafil and acetazolamide versus acetazolamide for the prevention of severe high-altitude illness.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>308-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1708-8305.2012.00636.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report an open-label study comparing tadalafil and acetazolamide (n = 24) versus acetazolamide (n = 27) for prevention of high-altitude illness (HAI) at Mt. Kilimanjaro. Tadalafil group had lower rates of severe HAI compared with controls (4% vs 26%, p = 0.03), mostly because of decreased high-altitude pulmonary edema rates (4% vs 22%, p = 0.06).</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2012 International Society of Travel Medicine.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
